  "text": "Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\ncompassionate cancer care\nFor your best friend.\nLearn more\nOne Cure\nThe answer to cancer may be walking right beside us.\nLearn more\nClinical Trials\nProviding hope for future cancer patients.\nAbout Clinical Trials\nNational Cancer Research Month\nLeading cancer discovery for 40 years.\nLearn more\nIf you are interested in scheduling an appointment with the cancer care team,\nplease fill out the appointment request form\n.\nOur team will be in touch within\n24–48 business hours with next steps\n.\nOne in four dogs and one in five cats will develop cancer in their lifetimes. Forty years ago, cancer was often a terminal diagnosis with few options, especially for pets. Today, there is\nhope\n.\nThe Flint Animal Cancer Center at Colorado State University offers a unique combination of advanced cancer diagnostic services, innovative treatment options, and compassionate care for dogs, cats, and other pets with cancer. As a patient, your pet will benefit from a\ncomprehensive assessment\nprovided by an expert, multidisciplinary team, including medical, surgical, and radiation oncologists. Each\ntreatment\nplan is customized, and we work with you to determine the right course of action for your pet’s care.\nWe are a world leader in the treatment of dogs, cats, and pets with cancer, with nearly four decades of experience in providing leading-edge, comprehensive, and compassionate care.\nOur goal\nis to make sure that you are fully informed about your pet’s diagnosis and\ntreatment options\n, so you are comfortable asking questions and making decisions. You are a key member of your pet’s cancer care-giving team. Our clinical staff is committed to helping you understand your pet’s cancer treatment and will offer support every step of the way.\nOur vision\nis to conquer cancer in all species.\nAbout Us\nOncology Care\nOne Cure\nClinical Trials\nResearch\nDonate\nYour gift is an investment in the Flint Animal Cancer Center's critical work to help pets and people with cancer. Together, we will find a cure.\nDonate Today\nnews and updates\nread more\nLatest News\nThe Flint Animal Cancer Center Advances Precision Veterinary Cancer Care\nKate Shelton\n|\nApril 22, 2026\nColorado State University’s Flint Animal Cancer Center has reached an important milestone in its mission to deliver world-class cancer treatment for companion animals. The new Varian Edge linear accelerator is now open and treating patients. This state-of-the-art system is designed to deliver highly precise and efficient radiation therapy for pets fighting cancer.  This advanced technology enhances the FACC’s ability to treat cancer with increased accuracy while minimizing impact on […]\nread more\nLatest News\nDr. Douglas Thamm named new holder of the Stuart Presidential Chair in Oncology\nKate Shelton\n|\nOctober 1, 2025\nIn a small but meaningful celebration, the Stuart Presidential Chair in Oncology passed from Dr. Susan Lana, the director of CSU’s Robert H. and Mary G. Flint Animal Cancer Center (FACC), to Dr. Douglas Thamm, oncology professor and director of clinical research at the FACC. The chair was first held by Dr. Stephen Withrow, the […]\nread more\nLatest News\nNational Nonprofit Petco Love Invests in Flint Animal Cancer Center to Save and Improve the Lives of Pets in Northern Colorado\nKate Shelton\n|\nJune 27, 2025\nFlint Animal Cancer Center (FACC) received a $100,000 grant investment from national nonprofit Petco Love in support of their lifesaving work for pets in Northern Colorado.  Petco Love is a national nonprofit leading change for pets by harnessing the power of love to make communities and pet families closer, stronger, and healthier. Since its founding […]\nread more\nResearch\nThe Ann and Lawrence G. Laiks Canine Cancer Fund Fuels Impactful Research\nKate Shelton\n|\nMay 16, 2025\nThe continued support of the Ann and Lawrence G. Laiks Canine Cancer Fund has allowed the Flint Animal Cancer Center to conduct impactful research that promises to make a big difference for dogs fighting cancer.  Lifelong learners and lovers of science, the late Dr. and Mrs. Laiks created the Ann and Lawrence G. Laiks Canine […]\nread more\nUncategorized\nThe Making of a Cancer Researcher\nKate Shelton\n|\nApril 28, 2025\nTreating cancer is not enough. We’re on a mission to conquer cancer in all species and, to make this a reality, we have to start at the cellular level. For more than 40 years, the Robert H. and Mary G. Flint Animal Cancer Center has been at the forefront of understanding the fundamental mechanisms of […]\nOur blog is dedicated to sharing the latest stories from the Flint Animal Cancer Center’s Very Important Pets & People! Plus, you’ll learn more about our current clinical trials, our inspiring research, and our work to find a cure for BOTH pets and people with cancer. We invite you to follow us!\nbrowse articles by topic\nFACC Patient Stories\nLatest News\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOne Cure Patients\nOur Team\nPet Cancer Treatment\nResearch\nStories of Hope\nTeaching and Learning\nbrowse articles by tag\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nCancer Research\nComparative Oncology Research\ncat cancer\nPet Cancer\nRadiation Therapy\nfeline cancer\nPatient Stories\nPatient Story\nPatient Assistance Funds\nFeline GI Lymphoma\nResearch\nVeterinary Radiation Oncology\nCanine Cancer Clinical Trials\nCanine Cancer Prevention Vaccine\nCanine Lymphoma\ncomparative oncology\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Staff\nflint animal cancer center newsletter\njoin our community\nBe among the first to learn the latest Flint Animal Cancer Center news and pet cancer treatment information.\nEmail\nThis field is for validation purposes and should be left unchanged.\nfirst name\nlast name\ne-mail address\nPatient Care Quicklinks\nAppointments\nOur Services\nTreatment Options\nFrequently Asked Questions\nPreparing for Your Visit\nConsult A Professional\nResources\nContact Us\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine and Biomedical Sciences\nCulture & Community\nVeterinary Health System\nSmall Animal Services\nEmergency and Critical Care\nFor Referring Veterinarians\nClient Information\ncontact us\nmailing address\n300 W. Drake Road\nFort Collins, CO 80523\nphone\nAppointments (970) 297-5000\nconnect on social media\nfacebook\nLinkedIn\ninstagram\nYouTube\nSupport the College\nApply to CSU\nContact CSU\nDisclaimer\nEqual Opportunity\nPrivacy Statement\nAccessibility Statement\nCopyright\n©\n2026 Colorado State University\nnavigation menu\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends",
